  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 339 PM CDT Sat May 23 2026\n\n...Synopsis...\n\nA mid/upper low is centered over southern Manitoba and moving\neast, with ridging upstream centered over the Northern \nRockies. The larger scale pattern will feature the establishment\nof a highly amplified ridge over the Northern Plains in an \nomega block type pattern. Ensembles show strong consensus in \nsome form of ridging holding through next weekend or even into \nthe week after, with more spread in clusters related to the \nweakening of the ridge in response to upstream troughing over \nthe Pacific NW and how that evolves. The scenarios that support \nthe ridge weakening in the west faster allow for a shift towards\nsouthwest flow as the trough begins to influence the region, \nwhich brings slightly lower (still above average) temperatures, \nand at least some potential for more active weather (daily \nthunderstorm chances) and a conditional risk for severe weather.\nA stronger ridge through the weekend (favored by more clusters)\nkeeps well above average temperatures and dry conditions in \nplace. Fire weather impacts are uncertain due to recent rains on\nwhether they have a positive influence longer term on fuels and\ngreen-up. \n\n...Thunderstorm potential Sunday afternoon/evening...\n\nAs the mid level trough transitions east and ridging builds aloft \nfrom the west there will be a transition that may allow for a weak \nmountain wave/shortwave trough to propagate along the US/Canada \nborder late Sunday. Earlier timing as NAM and some CAMs show would \nsupport a window where at least some forcing aligns with surface \nconvergence along a baroclinic zone in our north during peak \nheating. Steep low and mid level lapse rates result in at least a \nwindow where elevated CAPE and DCAPE 1000 J/KG+ could develop while \neffective shear values exceed 30kt+. Uncertainty rises from the \namount of dry mid and low level air in place, along with variable \ntiming of this weak wave/feature. A few strong gusts or marginally \nsevere gusts may be the main threat outside of smaller hail and \nlightning if thunderstorms do develop during the favorable window \n(4pm-9pm). \n\n...Hot/summer-like temperatures early next week...\n\nThere is minimal spread in guidance regarding highs with even the \n25th percentile of NBM guidance in the middle to upper 80s Monday \nand Tuesday. 50th percentile values are already within a few\ndegrees of daily records on Memorial day and on Tuesday are at\nor above (daily records generally 90-93F). Temperatures remain \nwell above average through the rest of the week, though not \nquiet as warm late week (increasing spread due to less certainty\nof ridge strength).",
